{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Group.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Finset.Sort","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.separable_branch_le_positive_part","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.separable_branch_le_positive_part","kind":"theorem","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L145-L159","doc":"**…and a separable bound needs no branching at all.**  With `c` antitone, the support\n`{j < J : 0 < c j}` beats every support, and it is an initial segment.  So the reduction above\nis real but only ever applies to bounds that did not need it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.separable_branch_max_le_initial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.separable_branch_max_le_initial","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L136-L143","doc":"**The `J + 1`-branch reduction, for separable bounds.**  If the bound on a branch with\nsupport `S ⊆ range J` is `Σ_{j ∈ S} c_j` with `c` antitone, then its maximum over all `2 ^ J`\nsupports is attained on an initial segment `range k` with `k ≤ J`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.sum_le_sum_initial_segment","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.sum_le_sum_initial_segment","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L120-L134","doc":"**Initial segments dominate.**  If `c` is antitone on `ℕ`, any finite support `S` is\ndominated by the initial segment of the same size.  Induction on the largest element `a`: the\nrest of `S` lies in `range a`, so `|S| - 1 ≤ a` and `c a ≤ c (|S| - 1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.antitone_slope_not_sufficient","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.antitone_slope_not_sufficient","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L109-L116","doc":"**Counterexample.**  On the branch `u ≥ 1`, row 1 alone reaches `5/2` (at `v = 5`), while\nrow 0 alone stays at or below `-1` everywhere on that branch.  So the best one-row support is\n`{1}`, not the initial segment `{0}`, even though the slopes at the origin are antitone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.slopes_antitone","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.slopes_antitone","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L103-L107","doc":"The slopes at the origin are antitone: `row0' 0 = 2 > 1 = row1' 0`.  Stated without the\nderivative API: for every small step `0 < h ≤ 1/10`, row 0 is ahead of row 1, so its difference\nquotient at the origin is larger. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.row1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.row1","kind":"def","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L100-L101","doc":"Row 1: slope `1` at the origin — smaller, so the slopes are antitone — curvature `1/10`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.row0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.row0","kind":"def","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L97-L98","doc":"Row 0: slope `2` at the origin, curvature `3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.certificate_split_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.certificate_split_eq","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L88-L93","doc":"The whole certificate, summed over the coordinates of any finite index set, is the same on\nevery branch: support branching at a fixed ascent point cannot lower it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.affine_box_max_eq_split_max","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.affine_box_max_eq_split_max","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L76-L86","doc":"**The split buys nothing at a fixed `w`.**  For `U ≥ 1` the maximum of `g (x - w)` over\n`{0} ∪ [1, U]` equals its maximum over `[0, U]`: the extra candidate `x = 1` lies inside the box,\nand both endpoints of the box survive the split. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.affine_le_boxMax","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.affine_le_boxMax","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L68-L74","doc":"Every point of the box is dominated by an endpoint — the reason `boxMax` is the box maximum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.splitMax","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.splitMax","kind":"def","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L63-L66","doc":"The split per-coordinate term: `max` over `{0} ∪ [1, U]`, i.e. over the three candidate\npoints `0`, `1` and `U` (an affine function on `[1, U]` is maximised at `1` or `U`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.boxMax","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.SupportBranchingValidity.boxMax","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/SupportBranchingValidity.lean#L59-L61","doc":"The unsplit per-coordinate certificate term: `max_{x ∈ [0, U]} g (x - w)`, attained at an\nendpoint because the function is affine. ","signatureChunk":0}]}